# IWE project memory: line endings pinned so that git itself cannot break the
# byte-identity invariant between the two copies of the memory schema.
#
# On Windows, git's system config sets core.autocrlf=true. With nothing pinned,
# an ordinary `git restore` of one schema copy rematerialises it with different
# line endings from the other. That is real on-disk drift, invisible to
# `git status`, and it trips the drift check with a message about drift that is
# not semantic drift. A guard that routine git commands can trip is a guard
# people learn to bypass.
#
# Merge these lines into an existing .gitattributes rather than overwriting it.
# Only the paths the invariant actually needs are pinned, so this is safe to add
# to a repository with its own line-ending conventions.

.iwe/schemas/*.yaml           text eol=lf
knowledge/.iwe/schemas/*.yaml text eol=lf

# `sh` does not tolerate CRLF, and git executes the pre-commit hook on every
# platform, so the drift check and the hook must be LF regardless.
tools/check-schema-mirror.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/*                  text eol=lf
